Original commit body from f00cfc4be4dcecdd6b6354188f6f54f66808697c:

Propagation pairs a source and a sink that SHARE A CALLER. All five
tree-sitter analyzers passed the FILE pseudo-symbol as caller_symbol to
emit_module_attribute_refs, so an attribute source's src was the file
while the sink's was the function, and the two could never meet. Python
was unaffected -- its own WI-guhok helper already anchors to the
enclosing callable, which is why the identical shape IS caught there.

Measured on one JS file holding both shapes in sibling functions:

  os.hostname()       -> fs.writeFileSync   FOUND   (a CALL)
  process.env.API_KEY -> fs.writeFileSync   MISSED  (an ATTRIBUTE)

Same file, same sink, same claim; the only difference is which end
anchors where. After: 2 flows instead of 1, and the emitted edge reads
src=javascript:app.js:4-7:leakEnv:function.

SCALE: 21 of javascript's 50 derived taint sources are kind=attribute,
including the whole process.env / process.argv family -- the canonical
way a Node program reads a secret. None could begin a flow.

Resolved by LINE SPAN rather than walking tree-sitter ancestors to a
function node: span containment needs no per-language knowledge of which
node kinds are callables, so the rule lives once instead of in five
copies that drift. A genuinely module-level read still anchors to the
file, which is correct and asserted. A PARITY TEST enumerates every
caller of the helper and fails if one omits the anchor, so the next
analyzer fails a test rather than silently repeating this.

The tripwire that pinned the defect went red exactly as its docstring
predicted and is replaced by the positive assertion it named, plus one
asserting source and sink SHARE an anchor -- asserting each end
separately would still pass if a later change moved both to some third
symbol.

THREE OF MY OWN ERRORS ON THE WAY, each caught by measuring rather than
by reading, and each recorded because the fix looked complete before it
worked:
  * a scripted edit anchored on a pattern instead of the whole call put
    the new kwarg inside getattr(...) in rust and inside
    _extract_edges_from_tree in cpp. Reverted and done with exact edits.
  * the js_ts path index keyed on ABSOLUTE paths while the emit site
    looked up a repo-RELATIVE name, so the lookup returned empty and the
    anchoring silently did nothing -- the verdict was unchanged and
    indistinguishable from the fix not working. Both halves now live in
    one module (symbols_by_path_index / symbols_for_path) so neither can
    assume a format the other does not produce.
  * Symbol.span is a Span object, not a tuple; len(span) raised inside
    the analyzer and the pipeline swallowed it into an EMPTY analysis
    (0 edges) rather than a failure.

